For performant decompression, FSST12 is loading the codes of the compressed string in blocks of 4 bytes:
```
while (posOut+16 <= size && posIn+4 < lenIn) {
      unsigned int code, code0, code1;
      memcpy(&code, strIn+posIn, sizeof(unsigned int));
      ...
}
```

This leads to a tail length of either 0, 2, or 3 bytes. One byte is not permitted, as we need 12 bits for one symbol. 
Two bytes left means that there is one symbol left, while three bytes left means that there are two symbols left to process. 

The decompression logic so far has always assumed that there is only one code left. With this PR, the tail handling is more explicit and also covers the 3-byte tail.
